# Story docs source

The docs **Show code** block is how people learn the API. It must be the JSX or hook call they would paste into an app.

- Write real usage in `render`. Do not render a helper like `<FruitSelect />` or `<DrawerExample />`.
- Do not put labels, wrappers, or layout the user needs to copy in a decorator. Put that markup in `render`.
- If a helper is unavoidable, set `parameters.docs.source.code` to the inlined usage.
- Skip `{...args}`, `fn()`, and the story object (`render:`, `decorators:`).
- Keep snippets on `docs.source.code` in sync with the live example.
- Controls that hold a value (Input, Textarea, Checkbox, Switch, Slider, RadioGroup, Select, InputGroup fields) use `useState` with `value`/`checked` and a change handler. Display-only pieces (Card, Badge, Skeleton) do not.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — Storybook chrome
{ render: () => <FruitSelect id="select-fruit" /> }

// ✅ GOOD — what to copy
const [value, setValue] = useState<string>();

<Select>
    <SelectTrigger placeholder="Select a fruit">
        {selectedLabel}
    </SelectTrigger>
    <SelectContent>
        <SelectOption value="apple" onClick={() => setValue("apple")}>
            Apple
        </SelectOption>
    </SelectContent>
</Select>
```
